                </BinderItem>
                <BinderItem UUID="317ED01F-51A8-4B06-B9BE-8F211648DB7F" Type="Folder" Created="2022-02-28 21:11:51 +1100" Modified="2025-11-02 19:17:02 +1100">
                    <Title>Change of garments
Or, so thought Chilo. It was obvious he was right about so many things. While he believed in the facilitation of worthy prophecies, he often differed on what constituted worthy. Still, it had been a lean few generations, and Chilo agreed Kleoboulos’ side project was the worthiest prophecy on offer. It was, axiomatically, also the unworthiest.
And so Chilo went about his sagery determined to act as a foil to Kleoboulos’ enigmatic approach. Fuck aiming for the smallest intervention. Fuck systems theory. And yes, sometimes, fuck rational enlightenment. For example, was their own universe so bad? Was it as bum-fucked aa Kleoboulos claimed? Chilo thought not. After all, the scourge of democracy had never flourished anywhere in their universe, and, while it excelled in superstition, misinformation, conspiracy theories, greed, economic rationalism, corruption and, fundamentally, all other the kinds of stupidity, had it not that same seething blunt amalgam of cruelty which produced an unparalleled social order, where the strong prospered and the weak were left in the wilderness to wither? It was beautiful in its militaristic authoritarianism.
Sure, there had been some heinous actors throughout history, Chilo thought. Dewey Dunning-Kruger (one-time President of the Squabbling States of America), Jamison Hardy, Linus Grayling (founder of Grayling Corporation), and, more recently, Eloise Shakti, Look at it this way: sure, Grayling Corporation reinvented slavery when they sold ELVIS clones, but look how it turned out: we’ve got more Elvises than any other universe in the history of the multiverse. Like most authoritarian social order characters in the brief history of time-wasting, Chilo could savour any flavour of evil, as long as it was in the name of maintaining stability.

Through skinny streets and alleys jagging among tangles of broken buildings bandaged by tired tarpaulins bound loosely with communication cables and power lines, a commando squad of Elvi escorted Tony and Sarah deeper into the heart of the Pale. With scouts ahead and sweepers behind, with four or five others flanking a perimeter, the squad combed the way forward as assiduously as they would comb their hair.
Sarah thought the Elvi were over-reacting, or at least over-acting which, in the aftermath of the gangster-style showdown in the Memphis Mafia Bar, dissolved none of her fear.
Tony, beholding the exquisite visage of Sarah growing ever more aghast amid she squalor and grime of the Pale, wall-papered the load-bearing walls of his own emotional landscape with a carefree veneer of detachment. With little else to say, he explained, ‘The heart of the Pale swallowed all the roads years ago, only narrow paths and tunnels are left.’
‘That sounds delightfully urban.’ Sarah replied. ‘Where are we going, anyway?’
‘Vegas Show tonight,’ one of the flanker-Elvis whispered, as if hunting for wabbits.
Tony glowed brighter than a Christmas tree. ‘We’re going to a Vegas Show?’
‘The Vegas show,’ Elmer Elvis corrected. ‘It’s our momma’s birthday.’
Sarah gagged the question rising in her throat: How do clones know when their momma’s birthday is? Instead, she asked, ‘PAPER agents want us dead, and we’re going to a show?’
‘Safest place in the Ghetto tonight.’
‘I think those creepy wizard dudes are following us,’ Tony said.
‘They are,’ Elmer agreed.
‘You’re not concerned about them?’ Sarah asked.
Elmer grunted and grimaced.
‘What did they want anyway?’ Tony asked, gluing up another layer of, I’m Cool.
‘They want to get me pregnant.’
The Elvi patrol halted in their tracks and made side-eyes at each other.
‘They wanna make you a momma?’ Elmer asked solemnly.
Sarah shrugged. ‘I’ve had worse propositions.’
Tony stomped about, hoping it would put out his incandescence. ‘You can’t be—no—what the tax? I— you—they—what?’
‘Steady there, Sir Galahad. I decided years ago I could never be pregnant.’
The Elvi shifted on their feet, as if preparing for a fight. ‘Are you not the Holy Momma?’ one of them shot at Sarah.
Tony, seeing his chance to come to Sarah’s aid for once, said, ‘Sure, sure, whatever, Holy Momma, Holy Cow, Holy Hand Grenade, whatever you folks want. Just get us to safety. Where are we going again?’
‘Graceland,’ Elmer answered.
Tony was beside himself with glee. ‘We’re going to Graceland!’

‘Chilo, stop rushing ahead,’ Kleoboulos chided. ‘They’ll catch us following.’
The nearer they approached Graceland, the steelier the stares they received from ever denser throngs and clumps of Elvi, and Cloudborne in Elvi garb. Something big was happening; there was festivity in the air, and crowds milled around outside bars and clubs and drank merry.
For a few seconds, Chilo lost Sarah and Tony in the coagulating crowd. ‘There they go!’ he pointed. ‘Down that side alley.’
The side alley led to an open market square, where every store-front, tent and flatbed truck displayed the latest in Elvi fashion, which is to say, the same jump suits, leather, cowboy and military outfits they’d worn since before their emancipation. 
The three Sages loitered while Sarah and Tony were led into one of the less ramshackle stores.
‘Yer going to the Vegas Show?’ asked an ageing Elvi, while he picked his teeth and leant against a truck.
‘Excuse, me?’ Solo said.
‘The Vegas show, yer know—the biggest night of the year round these parts. Seems yer friends are fixing’ to go if’n they’re buying themselves some proper evening wear.’
‘Outfits…’
‘Yeah, outfits. You cain’t be going to Graceland looking like asylum escapees. Now yer could join yer friends in that tourist trap over there, but why would yer, if’n you can pur-chaise the ‘zact same high-quality merchandise for half the price, from my fine emporium here?’
‘You mean your truck,’ Kleoboulos checked.
‘Yes’n deedie.’
Kleoboulos shrugged. From a rack by the truck’s tailgate, he selected a jumpsuit of many colours.
‘That one comes with a free cape,’ the proprietor said.
‘Oh, I think—’
‘Yer taking the cape,’ the proprietor said with increasing aggression, while Solo selected matching leather jacket and pants, and Chilo opted for an army ensemble. ‘To do otherwise would be sack-religious.’
Kleoboulos blinked and capitulated. ‘Where is the dressing room?’
‘Yer standing in it.’
‘What?’
‘Look around.’
Kleoboulos looked around the market. Everywhere there were Elvi and Cloudborne of all genders stripping and trying on clothes. He took a deep breath, then stripped and dressed. The suit was a near enough fit, so he accepted it rather than undress again in the open. He threw his pyjamas in a nearby oil-drum fire.
‘Let’s take a look at yer,’ the proprietor said when the Sages had adjusted their garments to their underwhelming satisfaction. ‘Line up!’ From a box in the truck passenger footwell, the proprietor produces what looked like three large syringes.
‘What are those?’ Solo asked.
‘Epinephrine auto-injectors, in case of anaphylactic	shock.’
‘None of us…’
Change of garments</Title>
